  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged prevailing beliefs through public dialogue, using what became known as the **Socratic method**. His main techniques included:\n\n- **Probing questions:** He asked people to explain familiar ideas such as justice, courage, virtue, and piety, exposing assumptions they had taken for granted.\n- **Cross-examination (*elenchus*):** He drew out the implications of someone’s answers, revealing contradictions between their beliefs and prompting reconsideration.\n- **Professed ignorance:** Often called **Socratic irony**, his stance of not knowing encouraged others to explain their views and examine whether their confidence was justified.\n- **Definitions and counterexamples:** He sought clear, general definitions and tested them against particular cases. A proposed definition of courage, for example, had to account for more than bravery in battle.\n\nThese methods asked people to justify beliefs through reason, challenging the authority of tradition, reputation, and popular opinion. His central concern was how careful examination could help people live better lives.",
